d to get him admitted earlier  but were sent home  now they learn it's too late to save him  the father of five is dead on arrival  since he wasn't admitted to the hospital  or taken to its morgue his death is  unlikely to be counted  in the city's swiftly rising official  toll  well let's go straight to delhi now  where dw's amrita chima is standing by  amrita what is the situation like in  delhi today  the situation here rebecca is very very  difficult  we are getting sos calls from hospitals  all over the city  saying that they are running out of  oxygen they have just a few hours  of oxygen left in their hospitals in  fact 20 people died overnight in a  private hospital in delhi  simply because the hospital ran out of  oxygen people who want critical care  equipment just died because the hospital  didn't have any oxygen  left in its stores now of course there's  a great sense of desperation and anguish  in delhi right now there are people the  case the number of cases are spreading  so fast that possibly there's not a  single family in delhi  which has not been directly or  indirectly affected  by the rise in cases in this second  surge  and there's also rebecca a growing sense  of anger  as to why this has been dealt with the  way it has why isn't more oxygen  available  yeah the scenes certainly are horrific  i'm rita um  as we just saw in that report the first  trains delivering oxygen is there hope  that conditions  might improve soon well apart from the  the oxygen that you saw  are coming by train in your report  there's also oxygen coming by road  because you have various highways which  have been cleared  and declared as oxygen corridors to get  these oxygen tankers from different  parts of the country  to go to destinations where they are  most critically needed  at the same time you also have some air  force cargo planes  which are bringing oxygen cylinders to  places where they are most urgently  needed  so help is on the way but the second  problem r